<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Danny Boy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1910">1910</a>; set to &quot;Londonderry Air&quot; in <a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The words of &quot;Danny Boy&quot; were written in 1910 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath, Somerset</a>, by the English lawyer and lyricist Frederic Weatherly, first to a tune other than the one now sung. The melody, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Londonderry</a> Air&quot;, is a traditional Irish air; Jane Ross of Limavady is credited with collecting it in the mid-19th century from a musician she met. How words and air came together is told in more than one way.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>One account holds that Frederic Weatherly's sister-in-law, Margaret Enright Weatherly, known as Jess, sent him a copy of the Londonderry Air in 1913, and that he changed his words to fit its rhyme and metre. Another has Jess singing the air to him in 1912 with other lyrics. A third says that Weatherly set the poem to no tune at all, and that in 1913 Jess, living with her husband Edward Weatherly at the Neosho mine near Ouray, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, set it to the air she had heard as a child in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, played by her father and other Irish railway workers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song is addressed to Danny as the pipes call him away, with the promise that the singer will wait for his return in summer or winter. Its second verse imagines him coming back to find the singer dead, kneeling at the grave to say an Ave, and the singer sleeping in peace until he comes. Its meaning has been the subject of conjecture; some read it as a parent's message to a son going off to war. The 1918 sheet music carried alternative words, Eily Dear, to be sung by a man, though the same words are commonly sung by women and men alike.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Weatherly gave the song to the vocalist Elsie Griffin, who made it one of the most popular songs of the new century, and Ernestine Schumann-Heink made its first recording. Percy Grainger adapted the melody for wind ensemble in 1918, and in 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht">Bertolt Brecht</a> wrote his own words to it as Erinnerung an die Marie A. The song is popular at funerals, though the National Catholic Reporter wrote in 2001 that it cannot be played during Mass.</p><p>It has been recorded many times, by Judy Garland and Glenn Miller in 1940, Bing Crosby in 1941, Elvis Presley in 1976 and Johnny Cash in 2002. Harry Belafonte's live version of 1957 opened with a spoken passage about Ireland's struggle and altered the words, making it more explicitly an anti-war song, and Conway Twitty's version of 1959 was banned by the BBC.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Danny Boy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
